course: dictionary
lesson: 11520
title: Allegation: a claim not yet proved
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An allegation is a claim that someone has done something wrong or illegal, said without proof yet. «Ruby made an allegation that Tom had taken her pencil case.» Ruby is saying Tom did it, but nobody has shown that it is true yet.
explain: Look for the clues. The sentence says Ruby made the claim, and the word «allegation» sits next to «that», which starts what she is claiming. Try swapping in your meaning: «Ruby made a claim that Tom had taken her pencil case.» It still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: Allegations are checked. «The head teacher will look into the allegations of bullying.» Teachers, police and courts find out whether an allegation is true. Until then, the person accused can «deny the allegation», which means they say it is not true.
fact: An allegation is a claim that someone did something wrong, not yet proved.
fact: «Allegations» of cheating, bullying or crime are checked by teachers, police or courts.
fact: Phrases to know: «make an allegation», «deny the allegation», «a serious allegation».
fact: If a wrongdoing is only an allegation, the reader knows it is claimed but not proved.
fact: A good check is to swap in «claim» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Ruby made an allegation that Tom had taken her pencil case.» What is an allegation? | A proof that someone is innocent | *A claim that someone did something wrong, not yet proved | A prize given for good work | A type of pencil case
exercise: «Tom said the allegation was completely untrue.» What did Tom do with the allegation? | Admitted it was true | Ran away from school | Asked for a prize | *Denied it, saying it was not true
exercise: «The police will look into the allegations of cheating.» What will the police do with the allegations? | Make them into a game | *Check them to find out the truth | Ignore them forever | Write them into a poem
test: «The school received a serious allegation about the broken window.» What does serious allegation suggest? | A reward for good behaviour | *An important claim of wrongdoing that must be checked | A small joke | A list of people's favourite lessons
test: Which phrase means that someone says a person is not guilty of a claim? | a serious allegation | make an allegation | *deny the allegation | allegations of bullying
exam: Which sentence shows that an allegation has not been proved? | *Ruby made an allegation that Tom stole, and the teachers will investigate it. | Tom won the prize for the best story. | The window was mended, and the class was happy. | Everyone agreed the boy was guilty, so the case was closed.
